1
00:00:01,218 --> 00:00:07,263
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thinking about anger, why is it wrong?

2
00:00:09,165 --> 00:00:10,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it unwholesome?

3
00:00:10,746 --> 00:00:15,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Why can't I just get angry to sort out a problem that has arisen?

4
00:00:16,091 --> 00:00:17,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone's doing something wrong.

5
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:19,694
[SPEAKER_00]: He's being unfair to me.

6
00:00:20,014 --> 00:00:24,598
[SPEAKER_00]: So, me getting angry and telling him, showing him... Why is that wrong?

7
00:00:24,778 --> 00:00:25,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, why?

8
00:00:25,739 --> 00:00:26,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because...

9
00:00:26,319 --> 00:00:27,680
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm practicing full nirvana.

10
00:00:27,861 --> 00:00:28,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

11
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:32,894
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot... well, anyway, it's wrong in itself, even if you don't practice nirvāṇa.

12
00:00:33,074 --> 00:00:36,055
[SPEAKER_01]: If something is wrong and unwholesome, it means it contains a contradiction.

13
00:00:36,975 --> 00:00:37,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

14
00:00:37,315 --> 00:00:41,536
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a certain discrepancy, there's a certain wrong assumption in the nature of that thing.

15
00:00:41,656 --> 00:00:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's unwholesome.

16
00:00:42,896 --> 00:00:47,117
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's the... that's what's in common to everything that's unwholesome in this life.

17
00:00:48,378 --> 00:00:54,579
[SPEAKER_00]: What unwholesome means that there is a discrepancy, there's a contradiction.

18
00:00:54,779 --> 00:00:54,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

19
00:00:56,019 --> 00:00:58,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, if you were to see, that thing would cease.

20
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:07,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were to see the discrepancy of anger, the contradiction of anger, you would have to cease.

21
00:01:07,502 --> 00:01:08,463
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be free from anger.

22
00:01:09,263 --> 00:01:19,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, when I say removing the anger, it doesn't mean it would disappear overnight, because that underlying discrepancy is basically because of your attitude.

23
00:01:20,666 --> 00:01:22,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's your view that causes that discrepancy.

24
00:01:22,886 --> 00:01:28,588
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your view that's the result of you being angry and and prone to anger or subjected to anger.

25
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:31,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it will take time for that to undo that.

26
00:01:32,602 --> 00:01:38,406
[SPEAKER_01]: But, the more you start seeing the discrepancy, the less fuel for it you're providing.

27
00:01:39,627 --> 00:01:41,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eventually, that fuel will have to run out.

28
00:01:43,069 --> 00:01:44,510
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the discrepancy?

29
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:45,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

30
00:01:45,090 --> 00:01:56,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the discrepancy in anger, regardless of whether you want to practice for nibbāna or not, is the fact that you cannot be angry, have the mind of anger,

31
00:01:57,527 --> 00:02:06,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Without implicitly, tacitly assuming that it's caused by the external factor, a thing or a person.

32
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:11,482
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is there a discrepancy, you might ask?

33
00:02:11,522 --> 00:02:14,444
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, like, what's the other counterpart that would make that a discrepancy?

34
00:02:14,504 --> 00:02:17,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, seemingly, anger is because you said something to me, so I'm angry with you.

35
00:02:18,387 --> 00:02:20,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Or something doesn't work, so I get angry with it.

36
00:02:21,490 --> 00:02:32,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But, see, that's the view, that the anger is in these external things, while, actually, anger is in you resisting the discomfort of those external things.

37
00:02:33,719 --> 00:02:37,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's one thing on the bottom and another thing on the surface.

38
00:02:37,762 --> 00:02:39,864
[SPEAKER_01]: On the surface, it's, yes, you made me angry.

39
00:02:40,544 --> 00:02:45,227
[SPEAKER_01]: But on the bottom, it's actually, uh, I'm angry with my own feelings, so to speak.

40
00:02:46,148 --> 00:02:51,652
[SPEAKER_01]: I refuse, I resist my own feelings, which is why I have this anger, which is why I assume it's rooted in you and it's your fault.

41
00:02:51,892 --> 00:02:52,993
[SPEAKER_00]: So you think

42
00:02:54,942 --> 00:02:56,203
[SPEAKER_00]: That you...

43
00:02:56,823 --> 00:03:02,507
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I'm angry because of you, because of what you have done, because of what the situation... Yeah.

44
00:03:02,987 --> 00:03:06,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, thinking why you're angry, that's... Let's just take experience of anger.

45
00:03:07,230 --> 00:03:08,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is anger present.

46
00:03:10,172 --> 00:03:12,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be with something external.

47
00:03:13,354 --> 00:03:18,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on that basic level, whether you think about it or not, and why, and so on, that's all secondary.

48
00:03:19,378 --> 00:03:22,640
[SPEAKER_01]: On the basic level, that presence of anger is already a contradiction.

49
00:03:23,802 --> 00:03:29,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is felt where you feel, but it's actually projected on this level of perception.

50
00:03:29,786 --> 00:03:35,209
[SPEAKER_01]: You saw unpleasant sight, perceived unpleasant smell, taste, touch and so on, thought.

51
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:38,271
[SPEAKER_01]: But anger is not in those things.

52
00:03:38,671 --> 00:03:42,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger can never be perceived in your senses.

53
00:03:43,194 --> 00:03:44,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is always on the emotional level.

54
00:03:44,735 --> 00:03:44,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

55
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:46,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where it's rooted.

56
00:03:47,017 --> 00:03:52,280
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in you resisting the discomfort of the unpleasant feeling.

57
00:03:54,785 --> 00:04:05,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you take that resistance of that discomfort, whatever unpleasant feeling it is, caused by this, caused by that, you take that discomfort at face value, means you're angry with the world.

58
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:08,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you're certainly liable to be angry with the world.

59
00:04:09,675 --> 00:04:18,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not give in to proliferating that kind of blame towards the external circumstances, but you will certainly be liable to it.

60
00:04:19,002 --> 00:04:21,984
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might keep yourself in check, but it doesn't mean you're uprooting the anger.

61
00:04:23,372 --> 00:04:25,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the resistance towards...

62
00:04:25,773 --> 00:04:27,374
[SPEAKER_01]: The resistance towards the unpleasant feeling.

63
00:04:27,434 --> 00:04:28,614
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of every anger.

64
00:04:29,274 --> 00:04:38,078
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't resist the discomfort, uncomfortable feeling, if you allow it to endure and don't try to get rid of it, it's impossible for you to be angry.

65
00:04:39,178 --> 00:04:40,679
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you're still uncomfortable.

66
00:04:41,079 --> 00:04:41,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, of course.

67
00:04:41,619 --> 00:04:42,319
[SPEAKER_00]: There's still a discomfort.

68
00:04:42,339 --> 00:04:43,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But anger was never discomfort.

69
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:45,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger was never in discomfort.

70
00:04:45,201 --> 00:04:46,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Discomfort was never the problem.

71
00:04:46,761 --> 00:04:49,702
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in you not wanting it, wanting it and trying to resist it.

72
00:04:50,162 --> 00:04:51,403
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, see, that's...

73
00:04:54,607 --> 00:05:01,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the anger is never in the discomfort, but it's your attitude towards...

74
00:05:01,949 --> 00:05:02,690
[SPEAKER_00]: The discomfort?

75
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:03,630
[SPEAKER_01]: That discomfort.

76
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:04,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is a result of that.

77
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:18,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, removing that wrong attitude... Yeah, basically not cultivating the wrong attitude towards the discomfort of the emotion that you might be feeling, that's... that will result in you having... removing the basis for getting angry.

78
00:05:20,216 --> 00:05:23,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, compared to like... obviously, it's a slightly...

79
00:05:26,676 --> 00:05:27,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Simple example, so to speak.

80
00:05:27,937 --> 00:05:35,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say you're really weak physically, really fair, really sort of... you can't bear much heat.

81
00:05:36,401 --> 00:05:36,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensitive.

82
00:05:37,262 --> 00:05:38,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Very sensitive in every way.

83
00:05:39,623 --> 00:05:44,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even the slight, like, change in temperature, and you, you know, you're just overwhelmed.

84
00:05:45,346 --> 00:05:48,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you know, whether you get angry on account of it or not, you're overwhelmed.

85
00:05:49,108 --> 00:05:54,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You're being overwhelmed by the uncomfortable feelings that arise from these discomfortable perceptions.

86
00:05:55,432 --> 00:05:56,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

87
00:05:57,301 --> 00:06:12,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And yes, person without trained mind, when they're subjected to external discomfort, they become irritable, they become angry, they start lashing out on random things and people, just because, you know, it's too cold, or too hot, or too hard, or too hungry, or not... haven't slept enough.

88
00:06:12,916 --> 00:06:18,502
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it takes, to basically shake up people, sort of,

89
00:06:20,648 --> 00:06:23,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental states that they think they're in control of.

90
00:06:23,890 --> 00:06:30,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Just expose yourself to a bit of elements and you'll see how quickly you're gonna lose control over your mind states.

91
00:06:31,354 --> 00:06:35,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're never trained to not lose control of your mind states.

92
00:06:36,757 --> 00:06:41,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, somebody very sensitive needs very little and and that's it.

93
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:42,481
[SPEAKER_01]: He's already overwhelmed.

94
00:06:43,521 --> 00:06:49,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine now, you go used to the heat and cold and hunger and really, like, it doesn't bother you at all.

95
00:06:50,086 --> 00:06:52,868
[SPEAKER_01]: That discomfort is not perceived as something you need to get rid of.

96
00:06:54,189 --> 00:06:55,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, it becomes inconceivable.

97
00:06:55,470 --> 00:06:57,252
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you want to get rid of it is when you're weak.

98
00:06:57,832 --> 00:06:58,613
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you're bothered.

99
00:06:59,093 --> 00:07:00,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why would you want to get rid of it when you're not bothered?

100
00:07:02,136 --> 00:07:06,639
[SPEAKER_01]: When you don't feel heat and cold on your skin anymore, because you conditioned it, so to speak.

101
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:09,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you wouldn't try to get rid of it.

102
00:07:09,902 --> 00:07:16,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Not trying to get rid of it, you would not suffer on account of it.

103
00:07:17,299 --> 00:07:20,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would certainly not become irritable and angry, because there's nothing to get rid of.

104
00:07:22,083 --> 00:07:34,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like when Buddha said that those underlying tendencies, those kilesas, to be overcome, to be destroyed by enduring.

105
00:07:36,175 --> 00:07:38,917
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, people might think, okay, this is this little niche.

106
00:07:39,018 --> 00:07:41,079
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually, most of it will boil down to that.

107
00:07:41,420 --> 00:07:43,782
[SPEAKER_01]: The very first thing you endure is sense restraint.

108
00:07:44,555 --> 00:07:46,676
[SPEAKER_01]: pains of renunciation, as we spoke before.

109
00:07:46,957 --> 00:07:48,297
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already a level of enduring.

110
00:07:50,138 --> 00:07:54,841
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, oh, I endure this and then I'll fix the problem and then I'll go back to everything else.

111
00:07:55,622 --> 00:07:58,744
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like... your enduring is undermined by the wrong view.

112
00:07:59,945 --> 00:08:03,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Your view should be, I endure whatever arises for as long as it arises.

113
00:08:04,447 --> 00:08:04,948
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't even

114
00:08:06,057 --> 00:08:11,858
[SPEAKER_01]: I hope, on the level of the view, that I can go back to it, after I endured it.

115
00:08:11,918 --> 00:08:21,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about enduring... just about anything, enduring the weather and becoming a hard person, you know, non-sensitive.

116
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:24,361
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to know what, why... Well, exactly.

117
00:08:24,401 --> 00:08:26,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about blind enduring or blind asceticism.

118
00:08:27,282 --> 00:08:30,462
[SPEAKER_01]: But, for some people, weather might be exactly that.

119
00:08:31,702 --> 00:08:34,143
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's not specifically what you endure,

120
00:08:34,913 --> 00:08:36,776
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like where your reasons are rooted in.

121
00:08:37,617 --> 00:08:38,779
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the underlying view?

122
00:08:38,899 --> 00:08:40,241
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a kind of a different topic.

123
00:08:40,962 --> 00:08:42,544
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to start enduring.

124
00:08:43,125 --> 00:08:45,027
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna have wrong views one way or the other.

125
00:08:45,448 --> 00:08:46,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to start enduring.

126
00:08:48,290 --> 00:08:51,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need to go and live on top of a mountain and freeze to death.

127
00:08:51,513 --> 00:08:55,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Just start practicing sense restraint and you have more than you can handle enduring.

128
00:08:56,957 --> 00:09:07,846
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is the enduring, that you are subjected to the senses, to the pull of the senses, you're subjected to that external world, but now you're enduring it, you're not cultivating your attitude towards it.

129
00:09:08,527 --> 00:09:11,670
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you'll eventually get to see that it's all rooted

130
00:09:12,352 --> 00:09:20,234
[SPEAKER_01]: In you, simply, automatically, blindly, ignorantly, resisting uncomfortable feelings and indulging comfortable feelings.

131
00:09:20,534 --> 00:09:21,974
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no reason for that, by the way.

132
00:09:22,795 --> 00:09:23,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is gratuitous.

133
00:09:25,155 --> 00:09:26,896
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no reason that you give in to lust.

134
00:09:27,716 --> 00:09:29,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that, that's why you give in to lust.

135
00:09:31,437 --> 00:09:31,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

136
00:09:32,097 --> 00:09:35,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is bad, because anger is always rooted in that discrepancy.

137
00:09:40,087 --> 00:09:42,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I resist unpleasant feelings.

138
00:09:45,690 --> 00:09:52,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's going... it can never free you from that fact that you are suffering.

139
00:09:53,674 --> 00:09:53,814
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

140
00:09:53,934 --> 00:09:57,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Anger cannot free you from being unable to endure.

141
00:09:57,556 --> 00:09:57,696
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

142
00:09:57,856 --> 00:09:58,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is the result.

143
00:09:59,437 --> 00:10:02,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, anger is obviously, you know, like a symptom.

144
00:10:02,639 --> 00:10:06,661
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you have an underlying condition that makes your skin itch.

145
00:10:07,441 --> 00:10:08,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, you scratch your skin.

146
00:10:09,832 --> 00:10:13,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, the underlying condition spreads and gets even worse.

147
00:10:14,293 --> 00:10:16,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anger is that scratching of skin.

148
00:10:16,113 --> 00:10:17,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is that scratching of skin.

149
00:10:18,074 --> 00:10:21,935
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the problem, it's the result of the problem, that it's aggravating the problem.

150
00:10:22,835 --> 00:10:25,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, you need to stop scratching first.

151
00:10:25,296 --> 00:10:26,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint needs to be established.

152
00:10:29,297 --> 00:10:34,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, to what extent you're going to take that sense restraint, well, that depends on your environment and how far you want to go in the practice.

153
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:41,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you really want to practice for freedom from suffering, freedom from sensuality, you have to be celibate.

154
00:10:41,863 --> 00:10:47,226
[SPEAKER_01]: That sensory strength needs to be taken all the way up to celibacy, which is the ultimate sensory strength.

155
00:10:49,327 --> 00:10:58,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you sort of, you know, that's a bit too much, certainly to begin with, you have to then start with five precepts and then practice sensory strength within that.

156
00:11:00,013 --> 00:11:02,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, oh, I keep the five precepts and now I can be careless.

157
00:11:03,315 --> 00:11:05,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And then every now and then take eight precepts.

158
00:11:07,489 --> 00:11:28,397
[SPEAKER_00]: The measure, the indicator that you are doing things rightly, you've said this before, the indicator which shows that you are doing things rightly, can be the the amount of chaos that you can tolerate.

159
00:11:30,038 --> 00:11:31,118
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, yes, exactly.

160
00:11:31,138 --> 00:11:34,660
[SPEAKER_01]: The amount of elements you can be subjected to without losing your composure.

161
00:11:35,496 --> 00:11:44,182
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can see it, I can endure all this chaos, fully composed, things are going well.

162
00:11:44,522 --> 00:11:48,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're certainly going in the right direction.

163
00:11:48,725 --> 00:12:03,496
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the main problem why people cannot see that the root of anger, root of sensuality, is in you resisting whatever present feeling it's enduring within you, the main reason they can't see that is because they habitually, automatically react to it.

164
00:12:04,237 --> 00:12:07,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Either with sensuality, or anger, or distraction.

165
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:12,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to stop that automatic, habitual reacting out.

166
00:12:14,504 --> 00:12:17,426
[SPEAKER_01]: And... and you stop that by enduring non-reacting.

167
00:12:19,668 --> 00:12:24,552
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if it's... if it doesn't feel like more... many more enduring, then you increase your non-reacting.

168
00:12:24,772 --> 00:12:26,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you tighten your sensory strain.

169
00:12:26,213 --> 00:12:27,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you take on celibacy.

170
00:12:28,354 --> 00:12:30,216
[SPEAKER_01]: You go, maybe I can't do it for the rest of my life.

171
00:12:30,276 --> 00:12:31,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, do it as a lay person for a month.

172
00:12:33,259 --> 00:12:37,742
[SPEAKER_01]: See what what strain your mind is under, then, and grow from it.

173
00:12:38,682 --> 00:12:42,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you'll see maybe it wasn't actually as bad as you as you projected it to be.

174
00:12:42,625 --> 00:12:46,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, it's necessary if you want to actually arrive anywhere in the dhamma.

175
00:12:47,228 --> 00:12:52,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Like even, as I said many times in the... you know, it's understandable that people kind of wonder...

176
00:12:52,171 --> 00:12:57,734
[SPEAKER_01]: I had, like, the noble disciples, sotāpāna, sakadāgāmī, they were still with sensuality living in the lay world.

177
00:12:58,154 --> 00:12:59,235
[SPEAKER_01]: They were after

178
00:13:00,317 --> 00:13:05,379
[SPEAKER_01]: They got their right view, after they got a degree of that liberation, not before or while.

179
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:23,287
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why even in the suttas, when the Buddha was teaching lay people, and they were getting the right view, he would teach them, as we said before, eh... generosity, rebirth, that whole thing, and then the peril of sensuality, the drawbacks, how little it's not worth, those pleasures must be renounced, the danger in it.

180
00:13:24,028 --> 00:13:26,609
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he would teach them the dhamma on top of that.

181
00:13:27,897 --> 00:13:35,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, sure, if they got the liberation, then they might feel like, okay, you know, this is... this is more than enough for me.

182
00:13:36,364 --> 00:13:37,605
[SPEAKER_01]: I have duties to attend.

183
00:13:38,385 --> 00:13:39,566
[SPEAKER_01]: And then go back to their life.

184
00:13:40,007 --> 00:13:43,689
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not to say that they arrived at that liberation through their lay life.

185
00:13:44,170 --> 00:13:46,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Lay life is incompatible with it.

186
00:13:46,191 --> 00:13:47,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Lay life of sensuality, that is.

187
00:13:47,853 --> 00:13:49,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like... not wearing robes.

188
00:13:49,674 --> 00:13:50,875
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not what makes you a layman.

189
00:13:51,516 --> 00:13:53,657
[SPEAKER_01]: It's whether you... you pursue sensuality or not.

190
00:13:53,797 --> 00:13:54,318
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what does.

191
00:14:00,072 --> 00:14:15,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even, as I said, even with all the wrong views or uncorrected views that a person might have, you want to basically start enduring sense-restraint, enduring uncomfortable feelings when they arise.

192
00:14:15,456 --> 00:14:18,997
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to seek them out, but you certainly should not run away from them either.

193
00:14:20,678 --> 00:14:23,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And, oh, you know, it's going to be a bit... Is it life-threatening?

194
00:14:23,839 --> 00:14:23,999
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

195
00:14:24,799 --> 00:14:25,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, why are you afraid of it?

196
00:14:26,779 --> 00:14:28,380
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to go far to feel

197
00:14:29,035 --> 00:14:29,515
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

198
00:14:29,675 --> 00:14:34,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there was that... in the... in other sutras, they said there are certain things that could be wisely avoided.

199
00:14:35,136 --> 00:14:38,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a wild elephant, wild dog, a tiger, snake, whatever else.

200
00:14:38,937 --> 00:14:41,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Things basically can result in killing you.

201
00:14:42,417 --> 00:14:56,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you recognize I'm acting out of fear here, but there is a good chance I might die, and I still am not enlightened, so I better not die, so that I can practice the resiberation, and later, if I come across a wild elephant, I might decide to not run away or something.

202
00:14:57,402 --> 00:14:59,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like you never sacrifice your intellect.

203
00:15:01,085 --> 00:15:03,867
[SPEAKER_00]: That's one of the main thing, main characteristics of the dhamma.

204
00:15:06,089 --> 00:15:13,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you shouldn't act out of fear, but it doesn't mean you sacrifice your intellect now and go blindly walk into fire because, you know, you must never act out of fear.

205
00:15:14,035 --> 00:15:14,215
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

206
00:15:15,516 --> 00:15:17,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You still keep your reason.

207
00:15:18,198 --> 00:15:26,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, this is out of fear, but if I avoid this out of fear, I might get to live and then I might get actually to understand and free myself from fear.

208
00:15:28,010 --> 00:15:36,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I don't, I'm just avoiding responsibility for my own actions projected on this goal, ideal of dhamma, and just walk into certain death.

209
00:15:36,855 --> 00:15:41,617
[SPEAKER_00]: As I was saying, you don't have to go anywhere to feel.

210
00:15:41,637 --> 00:15:41,917
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

211
00:15:41,937 --> 00:15:47,840
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to restrain your mind from going everywhere, and that's already going to cause you to feel a lot more.

212
00:15:48,901 --> 00:15:49,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, one of the reasons why

213
00:15:52,236 --> 00:16:00,242
[SPEAKER_01]: The quickest way to get in touch with your feelings, even in the, you know, new age spiritual manner, is start practicing sense restraint.

214
00:16:02,123 --> 00:16:03,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll feel much more.

215
00:16:03,905 --> 00:16:08,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Mundane things, daily things, you will feel them amplified, because now you're restrained.

216
00:16:11,521 --> 00:16:13,601
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you truly get in touch with your feelings.

217
00:16:13,661 --> 00:16:17,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by seeking them further out, or mystifying them even further.

218
00:16:18,243 --> 00:16:19,544
[SPEAKER_01]: No, by saying no to yourself.

219
00:16:20,604 --> 00:16:22,405
[SPEAKER_01]: It will make you feel immediately.

220
00:16:26,390 --> 00:16:30,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuous strength equals getting in touch with your feelings.

221
00:16:30,132 --> 00:16:30,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah?

222
00:16:30,532 --> 00:16:31,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

223
00:16:31,812 --> 00:16:35,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, sensuous strength equals you feeling the touch of your feelings.

224
00:16:37,155 --> 00:16:40,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not your choice whether you're going to get in touch or not.

225
00:16:40,256 --> 00:16:43,138
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to them, whether you ignore them or not, it's the same.

226
00:16:43,338 --> 00:16:43,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

227
00:16:43,698 --> 00:16:46,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Your feelings don't care about you.

228
00:16:46,799 --> 00:16:47,820
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't even know you're there.

229
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:48,440
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't care.

230
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:49,340
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't exist for them.

231
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:50,341
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to resist it.

232
00:16:50,721 --> 00:16:51,441
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to take it on.

233
00:16:51,461 --> 00:16:51,962
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to accept it.

234
00:16:51,982 --> 00:16:52,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that's indifferent to them.

235
00:16:53,002 --> 00:16:53,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings do not care.

236
00:16:54,063 --> 00:16:54,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

237
00:16:55,423 --> 00:17:00,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the discomfort that is there, will come, regardless of what you think about it.

238
00:17:00,449 --> 00:17:02,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:17:02,612 --> 00:17:03,633
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want it there or not.

240
00:17:06,977 --> 00:17:07,898
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

241
00:17:07,918 --> 00:17:10,121
[SPEAKER_00]: By just sitting still, not going anywhere.

242
00:17:13,024 --> 00:17:14,005
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be very unpleasant.

243
00:17:14,185 --> 00:17:14,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Five minutes?

244
00:17:14,726 --> 00:17:15,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Ten minutes?

245
00:17:15,407 --> 00:17:15,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

246
00:17:16,242 --> 00:17:19,563
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've never done this, you're going to feel like you're being overwhelmed.

247
00:17:19,743 --> 00:17:30,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, right there, discomfort, displeasure... Yeah, it's absolutely fine to use your reason and to recognize that, oh, I can't handle this right now.

248
00:17:32,307 --> 00:17:33,987
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a valid recognition.

249
00:17:34,007 --> 00:17:38,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so I can't just suddenly endure scorching heat and hunger.

250
00:17:38,468 --> 00:17:39,009
[SPEAKER_01]: I will die.

251
00:17:39,329 --> 00:17:40,469
[SPEAKER_01]: The body will shut down.

252
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:42,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's one thing.

253
00:17:43,021 --> 00:17:49,843
[SPEAKER_01]: And another thing is to then develop a view that I don't have to deal with this pressure in the boredom.

254
00:17:50,564 --> 00:17:53,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I can't, that's why I don't have to.

255
00:17:53,325 --> 00:17:54,805
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's an excuse.

256
00:17:55,686 --> 00:18:04,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize that you are unable, not strong enough, not trained enough to endure not having anything to do and not pursuing distractions and sensuality.

257
00:18:05,770 --> 00:18:07,891
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you dismiss that as a goal.

258
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:13,501
[SPEAKER_01]: To train yourself so to be able to endure it and not give in to sensuality.

259
00:18:16,102 --> 00:18:20,343
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we said a person needs to be developed in the body and in the mind.

260
00:18:20,383 --> 00:18:26,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as they're not developed in those things, they will be attracted to sensuality and pulled in anger as well.

261
00:18:27,986 --> 00:18:35,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So you will, by developing yourself, a certain necessary threshold of strength, so to speak, of self-control in that manner,

262
00:18:37,125 --> 00:18:44,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you will not be maintaining these discrepant actions of sensuality, ill-will and distraction.

263
00:18:44,768 --> 00:18:52,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And if, you know, we have other talks, people can search on our channel, where we talk about what is meant by this sense-restraint.

264
00:18:52,312 --> 00:18:53,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about saying no to everything.

265
00:18:57,354 --> 00:18:59,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Because saying no to every sight is impossible.

266
00:19:01,796 --> 00:19:06,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is meant by sense-restraint is basically saying no to things that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion,

267
00:19:07,135 --> 00:19:20,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And not saying no to things that are not rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, which means, oh, so you need to look back behind whatever you want to do, whatever you've been doing, whatever you're currently doing and planning to do, you have to look where is your motivation rooted in.

268
00:19:21,418 --> 00:19:22,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I saying this?

269
00:19:23,359 --> 00:19:24,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I doing this?

270
00:19:24,339 --> 00:19:27,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I performing this action, planning that action?

271
00:19:27,679 --> 00:19:33,181
[SPEAKER_00]: But you said a person can do that questioning.

272
00:19:33,421 --> 00:19:34,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, why am I

273
00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:39,202
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm beating this person up because it's the right thing to do.

274
00:19:39,743 --> 00:19:41,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, but that's not questioning it far enough.

275
00:19:42,304 --> 00:19:43,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels like the right thing to do.

276
00:19:43,545 --> 00:19:44,025
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know?

277
00:19:44,145 --> 00:19:46,607
[SPEAKER_01]: What is... where is your proof that it's the right thing to do?

278
00:19:46,747 --> 00:19:48,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Right thing means think free from contradiction.

279
00:19:48,688 --> 00:19:49,388
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels good for me.

280
00:19:50,189 --> 00:19:50,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

281
00:19:50,449 --> 00:19:52,830
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my... whatever it feels for me...

282
00:19:52,850 --> 00:19:56,752
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm happy that the person's being beaten up and getting his...

283
00:19:57,293 --> 00:19:57,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

284
00:19:57,593 --> 00:19:59,194
[SPEAKER_00]: ...vipakakamma is coming to him.

285
00:19:59,714 --> 00:20:01,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you choose to beat him up.

286
00:20:03,208 --> 00:20:04,049
[SPEAKER_01]: That's his vipāka.

287
00:20:04,429 --> 00:20:07,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody chooses to beat you up, you will be indifferent equally?

288
00:20:07,573 --> 00:20:08,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

289
00:20:08,133 --> 00:20:13,299
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be... actually, not indifferent, you'll be filled with joy, because you're getting what's coming to you.

290
00:20:13,319 --> 00:20:13,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

291
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:17,964
[SPEAKER_01]: But, see, so that's disingenuous, because you know that will not be the case.

292
00:20:18,925 --> 00:20:23,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you genuinely be filled with joy if somebody's beating and abusing you up completely?

293
00:20:25,513 --> 00:20:25,654
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

294
00:20:26,074 --> 00:20:33,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the view that, oh, no, this is joyful, this is the righteous action, is a contradictory view, because, oh, it's righteous if it's not applied to me.

295
00:20:34,458 --> 00:20:36,139
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's applied to me, then it's not righteous.

296
00:20:36,619 --> 00:20:37,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

297
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:44,783
[SPEAKER_01]: If you question things far enough, you will arrive at discrepancy, you will arrive at a contradiction, and then, well, basically, you have a choice.

298
00:20:44,883 --> 00:20:50,666
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either going to pretend you haven't seen it, no, there is no contradiction, or you will realize, okay, I need to change my views.

299
00:20:51,907 --> 00:20:53,368
[SPEAKER_01]: You can arrive at a contradiction,

300
00:20:54,753 --> 00:20:56,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Not ignore it, and not change your views.

301
00:20:58,035 --> 00:21:04,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot arrive at the understanding, recognition of anger, and then be angry.

302
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:12,305
[SPEAKER_01]: If you arrive at it, you'll either have to ignore what you arrive at it, in order to maintain anger, or if you don't ignore it, anger will dissipate.

303
00:21:21,251 --> 00:21:21,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, getting...

304
00:21:23,377 --> 00:21:26,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting angry to protect the dhamma.

305
00:21:29,359 --> 00:21:34,120
[SPEAKER_00]: That getting angry... Well, again, that's a contradiction.

306
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:44,283
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not... it leads to nibbāna, if you have the intent... You can only get angry in order to protect your idea of dhamma.

307
00:21:46,324 --> 00:21:49,145
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to bother and find out what the actual dhamma is,

308
00:21:50,113 --> 00:22:06,718
[SPEAKER_01]: What is closest to what dhamma has been, when the Buddha was teaching it, you realize, well, dhamma is that which is free from anger, free from sensuality, free from sexual intercourse, free from slander, free from lying, free from stealing, cheating, free from everything unwholesome.

309
00:22:07,238 --> 00:22:12,120
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I do any of these unwholesome things on account to protect dhamma, I am contradicting Buddha.

310
00:22:13,027 --> 00:22:13,367
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma.

311
00:22:13,608 --> 00:22:14,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the dhamma...

312
00:22:14,409 --> 00:22:15,169
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

313
00:22:15,189 --> 00:22:20,656
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can maintain a contradiction is to ignore the fact that... not push it far enough to see whether it's a contradiction or not.

314
00:22:21,657 --> 00:22:28,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting out of defilement is not freeing yourself from defilements.

315
00:22:30,126 --> 00:22:30,487
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

316
00:22:30,527 --> 00:22:32,449
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the result of you not being free from defilements.

317
00:22:32,469 --> 00:22:33,430
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot decide that.

318
00:22:35,850 --> 00:22:43,772
[SPEAKER_00]: I will just decide that acting out of defilement, maintaining defilement, will, for me, lead to nibbāna, that the Buddha spoke about.

319
00:22:44,352 --> 00:22:44,512
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

320
00:22:44,772 --> 00:22:44,892
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

321
00:22:45,212 --> 00:22:46,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's at odds.

322
00:22:46,052 --> 00:22:46,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

323
00:22:47,332 --> 00:22:55,574
[SPEAKER_01]: It can perhaps lead to whatever skewed idea of nibbāna you developed for yourself, but it cannot lead to that which the Buddha said nibbāna is.

324
00:22:55,654 --> 00:22:57,374
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, how do you know what the Buddha said?

325
00:22:57,394 --> 00:22:58,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, read the suttas.

326
00:22:58,614 --> 00:22:58,794
Yeah.

327
00:23:01,095 --> 00:23:01,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

328
00:23:03,085 --> 00:23:07,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, there were back in the day many people saying, no, this is nibbāna.

329
00:23:07,467 --> 00:23:08,607
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no, this is nibbāna.

330
00:23:08,747 --> 00:23:09,847
[SPEAKER_01]: They had their own nibbānas.

331
00:23:10,548 --> 00:23:11,608
[SPEAKER_01]: They had their own enlightenments.

332
00:23:12,989 --> 00:23:13,769
[SPEAKER_01]: And they were teaching it.

333
00:23:13,829 --> 00:23:15,469
[SPEAKER_01]: And they even had followers.

334
00:23:15,529 --> 00:23:22,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Followers who were like-mindedly ignoring, basically, contradictions of their own thinking, of their own intellect, of their own existence.

335
00:23:31,479 --> 00:23:36,501
[SPEAKER_01]: You always want to look for the universal nature of things, of your actions, thoughts and so on, intentions.

336
00:23:37,121 --> 00:23:45,203
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to just... Oh, no, this feels right right now, but I kind of know that universally, if this is always to be done, it's not actually good.

337
00:23:46,504 --> 00:23:47,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it's not good.

338
00:23:47,684 --> 00:23:48,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's a contradiction.

339
00:23:49,365 --> 00:23:51,405
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, it contains that discrepancy.

340
00:23:52,426 --> 00:23:54,987
[SPEAKER_01]: And dhamma is free from these contradictions in its nature.

341
00:23:55,547 --> 00:23:57,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So you recognize things as they are.

342
00:23:57,947 --> 00:23:59,128
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what that means, by the way.

343
00:23:59,933 --> 00:24:00,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing spiritual.

344
00:24:00,853 --> 00:24:01,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, as they are.

345
00:24:01,934 --> 00:24:02,094
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

346
00:24:02,234 --> 00:24:07,156
[SPEAKER_01]: As they factually are, free from any misperception that you might have placed there.

347
00:24:11,437 --> 00:24:14,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be finding truth on that universal nature.

348
00:24:15,038 --> 00:24:18,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you know... if you say anger is bad, anger is bad for everyone.

349
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:19,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

350
00:24:20,220 --> 00:24:21,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, not... you can't fudge it.

351
00:24:21,561 --> 00:24:24,202
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... You want to arrive at that universal understanding.

352
00:24:24,222 --> 00:24:24,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

353
00:24:24,602 --> 00:24:26,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is basically the principle of any philosophy.

354
00:24:26,782 --> 00:24:27,883
[SPEAKER_00]: At least, that's how it starts.

355
00:24:28,827 --> 00:24:30,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, they might go in different directions, but it is.

356
00:24:30,448 --> 00:24:33,451
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to arrive at the universal nature of it, which is universal.

357
00:24:33,491 --> 00:24:34,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Killing is always bad.

358
00:24:38,035 --> 00:24:47,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you don't know why that is, you can just adopt the idea that killing is always bad, or you can adopt the idea that killing is always bad, and then arrive at it yourself and confirm that killing is always bad.

359
00:24:49,544 --> 00:24:50,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is always bad.

360
00:24:51,085 --> 00:24:51,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

361
00:24:51,906 --> 00:24:53,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is always bad.

362
00:24:55,756 --> 00:24:59,039
[SPEAKER_01]: If something is unwholesome, means in its nature it's not good.

363
00:25:01,001 --> 00:25:02,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean that it's not gonna feel good.

364
00:25:04,665 --> 00:25:04,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

365
00:25:04,985 --> 00:25:06,967
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how it's felt.

366
00:25:07,007 --> 00:25:15,456
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, you want to arrive at understanding whether it's, in its principle, whether this is good or bad, if it's free from contradiction or not free from contradiction.

367
00:25:18,338 --> 00:25:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, we've done other talks, like, if you ask, so why sensuality then a contradiction?

368
00:25:22,941 --> 00:25:26,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're genuinely attracted to the ugly.

369
00:25:27,884 --> 00:25:33,067
[SPEAKER_01]: You have perception of beauty in regard to the disgusting.

370
00:25:33,607 --> 00:25:37,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing the disgusting because you're putting your perception of beauty first.

371
00:25:37,270 --> 00:25:38,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Then disgusting can come in secondary.

372
00:25:38,830 --> 00:25:41,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there is no beauty without the disgusting.

373
00:25:41,152 --> 00:25:42,553
[SPEAKER_01]: But there can be disgusting without the beauty.

374
00:25:45,014 --> 00:25:49,037
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's because of your view, you're attracted to that which is genuinely repulsive.

375
00:25:49,517 --> 00:25:52,519
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were not repulsive, the attraction would not be possible.

376
00:25:54,640 --> 00:25:58,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's repulsive, and it's impossible to ignore the repulsive, and that's how you become attractive.

377
00:26:00,824 --> 00:26:04,446
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's not repulsive also, freedom from attraction would not be possible.

378
00:26:05,936 --> 00:26:07,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you make an effort to see the beauty.

379
00:26:07,896 --> 00:26:08,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you are making effort.

380
00:26:08,897 --> 00:26:20,259
[SPEAKER_01]: You are selectively picking only, as the Buddha said, on the signs of beauty and ignoring the fundamental framework of those signs, as in even more general signs of those particular signs of beauty.

381
00:26:20,299 --> 00:26:24,940
[SPEAKER_01]: And those general signs are always ugly, always repulsive, always unattractive.

382
00:26:26,580 --> 00:26:34,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in itself, as I said, regardless of what you think, what your views of sensuality are, in itself, existentially speaking, sensuality is a contradiction.

383
00:26:35,168 --> 00:26:36,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is a contradiction.

384
00:26:36,528 --> 00:26:38,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Distraction is a contradiction.

385
00:26:39,449 --> 00:26:40,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Plus, the version delusion.

386
00:26:40,869 --> 00:26:48,351
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamental, basically, outpourings of the of the ignorance, for which every individual is responsible.

387
00:26:49,972 --> 00:26:55,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction is a contradiction, because you you want to distract yourself from that which you already

388
00:26:56,963 --> 00:27:03,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how can you distract yourself from something without that being the reason for your action, which means you're carrying it with you.

389
00:27:03,385 --> 00:27:06,106
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it because I want to get away from this.

390
00:27:06,126 --> 00:27:12,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not getting away from whatever you're trying to distract yourself from, because you're carrying it as the reason for your action of going away from it.

391
00:27:14,350 --> 00:27:22,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only true way of getting away from what's unpleasant on that level of your feelings is to not act out of it.

392
00:27:24,774 --> 00:27:25,854
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it will subside.

393
00:27:27,051 --> 00:27:28,073
[SPEAKER_01]: But not overnight.

394
00:27:28,754 --> 00:27:29,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why people cannot...

395
00:27:31,097 --> 00:27:37,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if sometimes they don't act out of it, they cannot see the connection between not acting out of it and things subsiding.

396
00:27:38,492 --> 00:27:42,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it takes repetition, over a period of time, of not acting out of it.

397
00:27:43,075 --> 00:27:48,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, you get to see that as the cause for things subsiding.

398
00:27:48,599 --> 00:27:53,582
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the... the sotāpanna, or so on, knows the escape from... Yeah.

399
00:27:53,642 --> 00:27:54,183
[SPEAKER_00]: He saw that.

400
00:27:54,223 --> 00:27:54,483
[SPEAKER_00]: That's...

401
00:27:54,503 --> 00:27:55,143
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows the escape.

402
00:27:55,163 --> 00:27:55,904
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the right view, yeah.

403
00:27:56,424 --> 00:27:59,246
[SPEAKER_01]: When you see the right... right connection of the right things.

404
00:27:59,706 --> 00:28:01,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean, before, I was saying, you cannot...

405
00:28:02,835 --> 00:28:09,903
[SPEAKER_01]: arrive at the right view of dhamma, because there is no dhamma before the right view, you cannot arrive at the dhamma, eh... accidentally.

406
00:28:10,563 --> 00:28:20,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can accidentally do one thing once, twice, three times, five times, but without any apparent discernible result, you won't be doing that thing.

407
00:28:21,815 --> 00:28:25,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you accept, eh... basically on faith,

408
00:28:25,641 --> 00:28:32,003
[SPEAKER_01]: The external instruction of the Buddha or somebody of his disciples that tells you, no, you should stick to it and keep doing it.

409
00:28:32,483 --> 00:28:42,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have no verifiable thing through your senses that proves that sense restraint will remove this sensuality, will make feelings subside and so on.

410
00:28:42,706 --> 00:28:45,887
[SPEAKER_01]: But you do it out of faith and because you do it, feelings will subside.

411
00:28:46,167 --> 00:28:51,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the simile of the Buddha and the man, the carpenter with the axe, who uses the axe to chop the wood every day.

412
00:28:53,330 --> 00:28:54,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And he can't come, like,

413
00:28:55,192 --> 00:29:00,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Today, and look at the handle and say, oh, yesterday was this much, now it's this much worn off.

414
00:29:00,193 --> 00:29:00,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Tomorrow it... No.

415
00:29:00,994 --> 00:29:03,614
[SPEAKER_01]: He just keeps you doing it, and not even think about it.

416
00:29:03,975 --> 00:29:05,815
[SPEAKER_01]: He's using it, and using it, and using it.

417
00:29:05,855 --> 00:29:06,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Practicing, practicing, practicing.

418
00:29:07,356 --> 00:29:10,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, one day, he walks in and says, oh, I have to replace this handle.

419
00:29:10,376 --> 00:29:11,257
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't use it anymore.

420
00:29:11,337 --> 00:29:12,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It's way too thin.

421
00:29:12,617 --> 00:29:15,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what used to make me suffer, I no longer suffer.

422
00:29:16,239 --> 00:29:17,679
[SPEAKER_00]: In the midst of this chaos...

423
00:29:17,699 --> 00:29:23,242
[SPEAKER_01]: In the midst of the same thing, same chaos, same thing that used to make me suffer, I don't feel anything now in regard to it.

424
00:29:23,722 --> 00:29:24,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a true freedom.

425
00:29:24,843 --> 00:29:29,765
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't feel anything, no pressure, there will be no burden to deal with the pressure.

426
00:29:30,846 --> 00:29:35,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're burdenless and free from needing to do anything, because there's nothing being felt.

427
00:29:37,133 --> 00:29:38,655
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the true peaceful feeling.

428
00:29:38,955 --> 00:29:40,778
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the true pleasure.

429
00:29:40,818 --> 00:29:41,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Diminishing feelings.

430
00:29:41,979 --> 00:29:44,082
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure of feelings.

431
00:29:44,122 --> 00:29:45,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Diminishing the pressure of feelings.

432
00:29:45,564 --> 00:29:46,725
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't diminish the feelings.

433
00:29:47,366 --> 00:29:51,491
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can diminish how much you're moved by those feelings by increasing your strength.

434
00:29:52,633 --> 00:29:52,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

435
00:29:53,714 --> 00:29:55,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people leave that unchecked.

436
00:29:56,485 --> 00:30:03,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And all the acts are aimed at dealing with the feeling, removing with the feeling, dampening the feeling, increasing the feeling, whatever else.

437
00:30:03,754 --> 00:30:07,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a contradiction of the same type, like anger and sensuality.

438
00:30:07,839 --> 00:30:10,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you think the problem is in the feeling.

439
00:30:11,123 --> 00:30:11,243
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

440
00:30:12,064 --> 00:30:14,628
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in you being weak in regard to what you're feeling.

441
00:30:15,379 --> 00:30:15,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Good or bad?

442
00:30:16,320 --> 00:30:23,864
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you're weak, when you feel pleasant, you cannot help but welcome it and detain it, delight in it, indulge in it and act towards more of that pleasure.

443
00:30:24,624 --> 00:30:32,089
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're weak, when you feel something unpleasant, you cannot help but try to get rid of it, deny it, ignore it, not have it arisen, basically.

444
00:30:32,109 --> 00:30:33,510
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of the discomfort.

445
00:30:34,370 --> 00:30:40,654
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're weak, when it's neither pleasant or unpleasant, you cannot help but ignore it and want to distract yourself.

446
00:30:47,753 --> 00:30:49,254
[SPEAKER_00]: So, face your feelings.

447
00:30:49,854 --> 00:30:50,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

448
00:30:50,235 --> 00:30:51,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Face feelings.

449
00:30:52,336 --> 00:30:52,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much.

450
00:30:53,597 --> 00:30:54,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop pressurizing.

451
00:30:54,957 --> 00:30:55,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings.

452
00:30:55,558 --> 00:31:00,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, you said something that's upsetting for me, for example, that's not the problem.

453
00:31:01,061 --> 00:31:02,882
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you can say it again, it's not a problem.

454
00:31:02,982 --> 00:31:05,464
[SPEAKER_01]: That you could, that you might will, maybe you will say it again.

455
00:31:05,824 --> 00:31:06,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, there's a problem.

456
00:31:06,885 --> 00:31:08,626
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that discomfort

457
00:31:09,473 --> 00:31:14,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It's now enduring, in my experience as a whole, on account of what you said, I'm implicitly trying to get rid of it.

458
00:31:15,074 --> 00:31:15,695
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm resisting it.

459
00:31:15,835 --> 00:31:16,895
[SPEAKER_00]: I do not want it.

460
00:31:19,395 --> 00:31:22,956
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop resisting it, you remove the basis for anger.

461
00:31:23,176 --> 00:31:28,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you repeat that sufficient amount of time, you'll see through it, and then you'll be incapable of becoming angry.

462
00:31:30,218 --> 00:31:35,259
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I said before, like, like sometimes people are, you just have to accept things that have a reason for you.

463
00:31:35,719 --> 00:31:37,260
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's true, partially.

464
00:31:38,137 --> 00:31:44,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when it's sensuality, opposite of anger, well, you're accepting it too much.

465
00:31:44,943 --> 00:31:55,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your rule of thumb is just to let everything be, and accept everything, and be one with everything, your rule of thumb, then, is to accept things that are already overly accepted.

466
00:31:55,312 --> 00:31:56,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is under-accepted.

467
00:31:56,693 --> 00:32:01,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you need to accept it a bit more, what you're feeling, in order to stop being angry.

468
00:32:02,302 --> 00:32:04,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But sensuality is overly accepted.

469
00:32:04,864 --> 00:32:11,290
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, you need to withdraw yourself from that acceptance, say no to it, in order, basically, to see through it, middle way.

470
00:32:14,433 --> 00:32:15,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the middle way.

471
00:32:15,554 --> 00:32:15,734
Yeah.

472
00:32:16,415 --> 00:32:19,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't just a blanket rule of thumb, I accept everything.

473
00:32:21,819 --> 00:32:22,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not.

474
00:32:26,684 --> 00:32:27,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that which is overly accepted,

475
00:32:28,542 --> 00:32:32,844
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not reacting... That's what the Buddha meant in those verses when they asked him, so how did you cross the shore?

476
00:32:33,725 --> 00:32:39,728
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, by not pushing forward, by not swimming backward, by not standing still.

477
00:32:39,748 --> 00:32:41,249
[SPEAKER_00]: By not standing still is the...

478
00:32:41,289 --> 00:32:42,029
[SPEAKER_01]: The neutral feeling.

479
00:32:42,309 --> 00:32:45,431
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you feel neither good nor bad, exactly.

480
00:32:45,451 --> 00:32:49,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't try to get rid of that feeling by distracting yourself from it.

481
00:32:53,655 --> 00:32:55,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in overly accepting, you don't accept.

482
00:32:58,182 --> 00:32:59,703
[SPEAKER_01]: In under-accepting, you accept.

483
00:33:00,843 --> 00:33:03,964
[SPEAKER_01]: In either accepting nor not accepting, you don't ignore.

484
00:33:05,404 --> 00:33:08,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't fight the waves, you don't pull backwards, you don't stand still.

485
00:33:09,826 --> 00:33:10,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, what do you do?

486
00:33:11,086 --> 00:33:15,148
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't fight the waves, you don't pull backwards, you don't... that's what you do.

487
00:33:15,897 --> 00:33:17,038
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that negative action.

488
00:33:17,058 --> 00:33:21,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You abstain from this doing on this occasion and that doing on that occasion.

489
00:33:21,479 --> 00:33:21,900
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

490
00:33:22,160 --> 00:33:23,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Only these three things you need to do.

491
00:33:23,620 --> 00:33:24,641
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the method.

492
00:33:24,721 --> 00:33:25,421
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing else.

493
00:33:25,841 --> 00:33:27,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so, is this rooted in sensuality?

494
00:33:27,802 --> 00:33:28,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I'll say no.

495
00:33:28,783 --> 00:33:29,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this rooted in anger?

496
00:33:30,023 --> 00:33:32,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I should say yes to this feeling, not to acting out of anger.

497
00:33:32,845 --> 00:33:34,005
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking on the level of feelings.

498
00:33:35,386 --> 00:33:36,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, is this... how do I feel now?

499
00:33:36,946 --> 00:33:37,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither.

500
00:33:37,907 --> 00:33:39,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither pleasant or unpleasant.

501
00:33:39,167 --> 00:33:40,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I should not ignore that.

502
00:33:41,568 --> 00:33:44,790
[SPEAKER_01]: I should not basically become stagnant in it, because I will sink.

503
00:33:47,818 --> 00:33:49,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the uncomfortable feeling.

504
00:33:49,679 --> 00:33:51,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so maybe I'm underly...

505
00:33:51,241 --> 00:33:52,802
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm under-accepting it, so to speak.

506
00:33:52,842 --> 00:33:53,663
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to get rid of it.

507
00:33:54,243 --> 00:33:56,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I need to accept the uncomfortable feeling for what it is.

508
00:33:58,006 --> 00:33:59,047
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the problem disappears.

509
00:33:59,627 --> 00:34:03,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there is a extremely comfortable feeling and prospect of increasing it.

510
00:34:03,230 --> 00:34:04,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I need to say no to that.

511
00:34:05,432 --> 00:34:09,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say no to under-accepted feeling, you're just doubling down in your anger.

512
00:34:11,537 --> 00:34:15,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you say yes to your overly accepted feeling of sensuality, you're doubling down in sensuality.

513
00:34:17,620 --> 00:34:19,461
[SPEAKER_01]: And how will you know where it is?

514
00:34:19,481 --> 00:34:20,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Look where your motivation is rooted.

515
00:34:21,403 --> 00:34:22,003
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you doing?

516
00:34:22,123 --> 00:34:22,904
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you doing for?

517
00:34:24,685 --> 00:34:28,407
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's the present enduring feeling while you're doing that or planning to do that?

518
00:34:29,888 --> 00:34:31,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want to go to such and such place.

519
00:34:31,389 --> 00:34:31,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

520
00:34:32,250 --> 00:34:33,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because some people they need to see.

521
00:34:33,951 --> 00:34:34,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why do you need to?

522
00:34:35,132 --> 00:34:35,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you not see them?

523
00:34:36,012 --> 00:34:36,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ask yourself.

524
00:34:37,710 --> 00:34:47,352
[SPEAKER_01]: If you question yourself far enough, you'll see, no, I genuinely need to do this practical purpose or whatever, or it will be, oh no, I'm just basically bored and I'm seeking company and I'm trying to justify it to myself.

525
00:34:48,253 --> 00:34:50,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so you're acting out of feeling and you're trying to cover up.

526
00:34:51,473 --> 00:34:52,114
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the reason.

527
00:34:53,834 --> 00:34:55,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what am I feeling?

528
00:34:56,535 --> 00:34:58,495
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my attitude to this feeling?

529
00:35:00,476 --> 00:35:01,996
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, how does that

530
00:35:03,138 --> 00:35:03,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I acting out of it?

531
00:35:05,020 --> 00:35:08,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm feeling this pressure, this boredom, you know, I haven't been going out for a while.

532
00:35:08,903 --> 00:35:10,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but why is that a problem now?

533
00:35:13,448 --> 00:35:15,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to recognize it's a problem, because you're being pressured to act.

534
00:35:17,091 --> 00:35:18,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, okay, I can't handle it.

535
00:35:18,913 --> 00:35:19,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to do something.

536
00:35:19,834 --> 00:35:20,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

537
00:35:20,655 --> 00:35:22,917
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't ignore the fact that you acted out of weakness.

538
00:35:23,817 --> 00:35:31,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by not ignoring the fact that you acted out of weakness, you do not devalue the goal of not acting out of weakness.

539
00:35:31,761 --> 00:35:33,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how eventually you will become stronger.

540
00:35:34,222 --> 00:35:34,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

541
00:35:34,503 --> 00:35:38,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be honest with yourself and accept when you are weak.

542
00:35:38,525 --> 00:35:39,285
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're weak, exactly.

543
00:35:39,305 --> 00:35:40,446
[SPEAKER_00]: That is not good enough.

544
00:35:40,486 --> 00:35:41,786
[SPEAKER_00]: No weak mind and weak mind.

545
00:35:41,826 --> 00:35:42,687
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not good enough.

546
00:35:43,127 --> 00:35:43,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

547
00:35:44,107 --> 00:35:45,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... okay, I just...

548
00:35:45,788 --> 00:35:49,469
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't handle it, so I went out and distracted myself.

549
00:35:49,669 --> 00:35:50,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, fine.

550
00:35:50,549 --> 00:35:55,710
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine in that regard, but it's not fine if you use that to not justify that you don't need to restrain yourself.

551
00:35:56,090 --> 00:35:57,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is nothing wrong in going out.

552
00:35:57,490 --> 00:36:03,352
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing wrong in being out, but there is wrong in going out on account of the pressure of your feelings.

553
00:36:05,732 --> 00:36:08,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in itself, very few things are wrong.

554
00:36:10,227 --> 00:36:14,748
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at the word motivation behind those things that are done, then everything can be wrong.

555
00:36:15,609 --> 00:36:17,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everything can be done for the wrong reason.

556
00:36:17,969 --> 00:36:19,010
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what makes you wrong.

557
00:36:21,150 --> 00:36:33,434
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you act out of weakness, you recognize that, and then you will... you still do not neglect, you do not ignore, you don't cover up, you don't devalue the value of your goal, to not act out of weakness.

558
00:36:33,814 --> 00:36:36,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, when you act out of weakness, the discrepancy will be felt how?

559
00:36:36,815 --> 00:36:37,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Like guilt.

560
00:36:38,097 --> 00:36:38,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Remorse.

561
00:36:38,837 --> 00:36:39,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Shame.

562
00:36:39,398 --> 00:36:40,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Shame, exactly.

563
00:36:40,418 --> 00:36:41,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a good type.

564
00:36:42,860 --> 00:36:45,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's from the point of view of what I value.

565
00:36:45,921 --> 00:36:50,063
[SPEAKER_01]: What is fitting for my values, as the Buddha would say, I acted like this.

566
00:36:50,103 --> 00:36:51,004
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not fitting.

567
00:36:52,064 --> 00:36:53,045
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I feel ashamed.

568
00:36:53,165 --> 00:36:53,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Good.

569
00:36:53,965 --> 00:36:55,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Allow that shame to endure.

570
00:36:55,826 --> 00:37:00,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Next time you're about to act against your values, well, you'll be more aware of what you're about to do.

571
00:37:01,580 --> 00:37:06,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you are aware of it, the less automatically, habitually, you'll act towards it.

572
00:37:06,824 --> 00:37:08,465
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, sense restraint will be easier.

573
00:37:08,525 --> 00:37:09,846
[SPEAKER_00]: You're always making things clear.

574
00:37:10,386 --> 00:37:11,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Making, oh, that's the goal.

575
00:37:12,167 --> 00:37:13,108
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the goal there.

576
00:37:13,128 --> 00:37:13,528
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the goal.

577
00:37:13,548 --> 00:37:14,809
[SPEAKER_00]: The goal remains unchanged, yeah.

578
00:37:15,149 --> 00:37:20,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing that can change about the goal is, you can become even clearer about it.

579
00:37:20,582 --> 00:37:21,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, even if you're failing.

580
00:37:22,162 --> 00:37:22,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're failing.

581
00:37:22,883 --> 00:37:23,543
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not a problem.

582
00:37:23,603 --> 00:37:27,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you ignore your failure, your failures, your weakness...

583
00:37:27,484 --> 00:37:37,206
[SPEAKER_01]: If you ignore your failure, means, okay, you maybe you're not neglecting, you're not ignoring the goal, but you're ignoring the fact that you're constantly contradicting it, which means there's no progress.

584
00:37:37,626 --> 00:37:46,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But the even worse thing than that is, on account of inability, basically, to not act out of weakness, so on account of your constant failures,

585
00:37:48,044 --> 00:37:51,393
[SPEAKER_01]: of acting out of weakness, you then reshape your goal.

586
00:37:52,195 --> 00:37:53,879
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, no, no, no, no, no.

587
00:37:54,020 --> 00:37:55,604
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to be sense-restrained.

588
00:37:56,222 --> 00:37:58,062
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, celibacy is only optional.

589
00:37:58,523 --> 00:37:58,943
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

590
00:37:59,103 --> 00:38:00,603
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha taught that back in the day.

591
00:38:00,643 --> 00:38:01,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That was applicable.

592
00:38:01,463 --> 00:38:02,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's not.

593
00:38:02,984 --> 00:38:09,945
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that, say, even if it were true, it's not for you, because it's rooted in you simply justifying your weakness.

594
00:38:10,785 --> 00:38:18,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Only if you are fully free from your weakness, already enlightened mind, then you can revisit what the Buddha said and done, and see if it's applicable or not.

595
00:38:19,527 --> 00:38:22,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Until then, it will be rooted in your own weakness, because you're not seeing it.

596
00:38:24,143 --> 00:38:26,864
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you could see sensuality is, you become free from it.

597
00:38:27,984 --> 00:38:30,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully understanding it, fully seeing it, you'll know the escape from it.

598
00:38:31,705 --> 00:38:39,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you now, saying, no, you don't need to be restrained, or you need to be restrained, or you don't need to do this, it's all just stabbing in the dark, and it has to be rooted somewhere.

599
00:38:39,688 --> 00:38:43,609
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's going to be rooted in that which you have, which is weakness, acting out of my weakness.

600
00:38:46,675 --> 00:38:54,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you first need to qualify yourself to revisit whether there is another alternative to be taught or the dhamma can be taught in.

601
00:38:54,640 --> 00:38:57,061
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't need to really look that far.

602
00:38:57,081 --> 00:39:08,908
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can revisit if you want, but it's pointless effort, because if you have even a degree of understanding of the dhamma, you will realize that, yeah, there's a reason the Buddha was called the foremost in formulating the dhamma.

603
00:39:09,208 --> 00:39:13,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Even previous Buddha were not so precise and eloquent and accurate like he was.

604
00:39:15,385 --> 00:39:17,987
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you think you can do away with those instructions?

605
00:39:18,408 --> 00:39:19,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why?

606
00:39:19,949 --> 00:39:20,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself.

607
00:39:20,990 --> 00:39:21,791
[SPEAKER_01]: It might make sense.

608
00:39:22,131 --> 00:39:23,112
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't need to do this.

609
00:39:23,793 --> 00:39:24,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, fine.

610
00:39:25,354 --> 00:39:27,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Now ask yourself, why do I hold such a view?

611
00:39:29,297 --> 00:39:30,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is that rooted?

612
00:39:30,218 --> 00:39:32,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it rooted in the fact that I'm free from sensuality?

613
00:39:33,081 --> 00:39:33,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not?

614
00:39:33,581 --> 00:39:33,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Not.

615
00:39:34,882 --> 00:39:39,967
[SPEAKER_01]: So could it possibly be that it's rooted in just me trying to justify my weakness because it's too unpleasant to deal with it?

616
00:39:42,470 --> 00:39:43,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

617
00:39:43,170 --> 00:39:43,931
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

618
00:39:44,731 --> 00:39:49,813
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say no, but again, there can be an arbitrary answer for which you will not have, like, support.

619
00:39:50,493 --> 00:39:52,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you question no, how do I know no?

620
00:39:53,034 --> 00:39:53,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't.

621
00:39:53,974 --> 00:39:54,794
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you do know?

622
00:39:55,255 --> 00:39:59,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I do know that that I do act out of pressure and weakness.

623
00:39:59,976 --> 00:40:01,837
[SPEAKER_01]: When sensuality arises, I can't restrain myself.

624
00:40:02,257 --> 00:40:04,618
[SPEAKER_01]: When anger presents itself, well,

625
00:40:05,389 --> 00:40:06,169
[SPEAKER_01]: I still feel angry.

626
00:40:06,550 --> 00:40:07,570
[SPEAKER_01]: I still hate that person.

627
00:40:07,670 --> 00:40:10,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I restrain my act, that's good, but I still hate them.

628
00:40:10,831 --> 00:40:17,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have a perspective, then, on whether you can do away with sense restraint or not, and to what extent.

629
00:40:19,075 --> 00:40:24,797
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, the vinaya, sīla, precepts, the whole thing, needs to be taken on first.

630
00:40:25,438 --> 00:40:26,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, later on,

631
00:40:28,049 --> 00:40:33,314
[SPEAKER_01]: When the minds were pure completely from greed, aversion, delusion, yes, you don't need to keep all the precepts.

632
00:40:33,455 --> 00:40:36,017
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are certain precepts that you cannot not keep.

633
00:40:37,379 --> 00:40:40,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is all the precepts that deal with... that are based in sensuality.

634
00:40:41,623 --> 00:40:42,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Or killing.

635
00:40:42,624 --> 00:40:43,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Or killing, exactly.

636
00:40:44,005 --> 00:40:49,111
[SPEAKER_01]: So, precepts, they were rooted around sensuality, anger, carelessness, these things.

637
00:40:49,747 --> 00:40:50,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Concealing truth.

638
00:40:50,828 --> 00:40:53,049
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to break those precepts, yeah.

639
00:40:53,069 --> 00:40:54,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But there were those precepts in between.

640
00:40:54,649 --> 00:40:56,330
[SPEAKER_01]: They were kind of a response to this and that.

641
00:40:56,690 --> 00:41:00,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And that sometimes people try to bundle the fundamental precepts, like the eight precepts.

642
00:41:01,913 --> 00:41:07,715
[SPEAKER_01]: They try to bundle with all these other arbitrary precepts of the kind of social structure and the time.

643
00:41:09,196 --> 00:41:10,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, don't wear your robe like this.

644
00:41:10,337 --> 00:41:10,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do that.

645
00:41:11,677 --> 00:41:13,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, no, those are earth and sky.

646
00:41:14,736 --> 00:41:25,362
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, if you were to do away with them, you can only do once you free yourself from all these underlying tendencies that would be resulting in wrongdoings.

647
00:41:31,866 --> 00:41:35,548
[SPEAKER_01]: But anything, any priest that has to do with sensuality, ill-will,

648
00:41:37,735 --> 00:41:41,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Harm, cruelty, that's not optional.

649
00:41:42,059 --> 00:41:43,440
[SPEAKER_01]: That needs to be taken beforehand.

650
00:41:43,801 --> 00:41:47,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you get to understand the truth, you become those precepts.

651
00:41:47,304 --> 00:41:53,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to keep the precepts of being celibate as an arahant, because that is your mode of being.

652
00:41:53,790 --> 00:41:55,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Sexual intercourse is inconceivable.

653
00:41:55,772 --> 00:42:02,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot keep the precepts of... You don't have to keep the precept of, I will not kill anyone, because it's inconceivable to intentionally kill anyone.

654
00:42:03,345 --> 00:42:04,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Or not to lie.

655
00:42:04,285 --> 00:42:05,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not lying, exactly.

656
00:42:05,326 --> 00:42:07,067
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to make the effort to not lie.

657
00:42:07,127 --> 00:42:16,751
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's inconceivable for you to lie, because you took on the values, the fundamental values, through sense restraint, through faith, through precepts, but then molded yourself into them.

658
00:42:16,851 --> 00:42:24,475
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you took the value of strength, you took the value of arahantship, the goal of arahantship, and then you haven't done everything that was preventing you from fitting in it.

659
00:42:26,016 --> 00:42:26,476
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

